<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Union Island is one of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenadines">Grenadines</a>, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a>, with its main village at Clifton. In December 1979, weeks after independence, a brief armed uprising there was put down with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>. In July 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-beryl">Hurricane Beryl</a> destroyed or damaged most of its buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-island.jpg" alt="Union Island" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unionisland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Union Island has an area of 9 square kilometres and lies about 200 km west-southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, within view of Carriacou and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, which lies directly to the south. It is roughly 4.8 kilometres long and 1.6 kilometres wide. Its volcanic outline has earned it the name Tahiti of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, and its highest peak, Mount Taboi, rises 304 metres above sea level. The climate is semi-arid, because the hills are not high enough to bring the rainfall that turns the northern coast of Grenada into rainforest. In the dry season, from December to June, the island depends on water stored during the rainy season. Nearby islands include Mayreau, Palm Island, Petit Saint Vincent and the Tobago Cays.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the original Arawak and Carib settlers, the island passed into the hands of French and English slave traders and plantation owners. They brought hundreds of Africans there, mostly from regions that are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and sea island <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> was an important export crop. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished, people still lived by farming and fishing, and many men went to sea to work on freighters to support their families.</p><p>In the late 1970s Union Island was the centre of political unrest, when a group of residents favoured seceding from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a> and joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, their neighbour to the south. The government of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines put down the insurrection with its forces. In July 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-beryl">Hurricane Beryl</a>, an extremely powerful storm, devastated the island, leaving most of its residents homeless and most of its infrastructure in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Union Island is part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a>, and Clifton and Ashton are its two principal towns. It has just under 3,000 residents, and English is the official language, with French and German spoken by some merchants in Clifton. The island has an airport with flights to Saint Vincent and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenadines">Grenadines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, Carriacou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> and Martinique, and ferries link Clifton with Saint Vincent. Yacht services and tourism provide many jobs, since many yachts call on their way to the nearby Tobago Cays National Marine Park. The Union Island gecko, discovered in 2005, is found only on the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weeks later a brief armed uprising on <b>Union Island</b> was put down with help from Barbados.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-vincent-and-the-grenadines">Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</a></li></ul></section>
